    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the confusion. Here's a more clinical rundown of what to look for in your heifer post-rumenotomy:\n\n1. **Temperature**: A fever above 39.2°C (102.6°F) can indicate an infection. Monitor her temperature regularly.\n\n2. **Rumen Motility**: Decreased motility or a lack of rumen contractions can be a sign of peritonitis or an abscess. Observe her rumen for any unusual sounds or lack of movement.\n\n3. **Abdominal Pain**: Palpate her abdomen gently. Pain or discomfort upon palpation, especially in the rumen area, can indicate an underlying issue.\n\n4. **Behavioral Changes**: Watch for signs of distress, such as restlessness, reluctance to move, or changes in eating habits. These can be early indicators of discomfort or pain.\n\n5. **Appetite**: A sudden loss of appetite or a decrease in food intake can be a sign of discomfort or pain.\n\n6. **Lameness**: If she shows signs of lameness, it could be related to an abscess or infection in the lower abdomen.\n\n7. **Drainage**: Check for any unusual discharge from the surgical",
      "source": "target",
